 "final_text": "I have identified and fixed the bug in the Quasi-Monte Carlo pricing of path-dependent products that was causing bias when using Sobol sampling with Brownian bridge.\n\n## Problem Analysis\n\nThe issue was in the `sobol_bridged_gaussians` function in `/workspace/project/include/quantModeling/engines/mc/sobol_bridge.hpp`. When generating Gaussian random variables from Sobol sequences with Brownian bridge, the digital shift was not being properly applied to the Sobol points.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nIn line 60 of `sobol_bridge.hpp`, the code was calling:\n```cpp\nreturn inverse_normal_cdf(sobol_to_uniform(bits, 0u));\n```\n\nThis was incorrectly passing `0u` as the shift parameter instead of using the actual shift value from the shift array. The correct implementation should use the shift value that was computed for each dimension.\n\n## Fix Applied\n\nI changed line 60 from:\n```cpp\nreturn inverse_normal_cdf(sobol_to_uniform(bits, 0u));\n```\nto:\n```cpp\nreturn inverse_normal_cdf(sobol_to_uniform(bits, sh[j]));\n```\n\nThis ensures that the digital shift (which is crucial for proper Sobol sequence randomization) is correctly applied to each coordinate, making the Sobol sequences unbiased.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe fix has been verified by:\n1. Running existing Sobol-related tests, which all pass\n2. Specifically testing the `SobolBridge.FlatPointEqualsTheGenericEngines` test, which validates that the flat point mapping works correctly\n3. Running the `BrownianBridgeEngine.SobolWithBridgeConvergesFasterOnAnAsian` test, which validates the Asian option pricing with Sobol and Brownian bridge\n\nThe fix is minimal and maintains full backward compatibility while resolving the bias issue described in the problem statement. Path-dependent products like arithmetic Asian options will now price consistently whether `mc_brownian_bridge` is true or false, and the disagreement with more paths will no longer occur.",
